# Interaction preferences

## Never use the AskQuestion tool

Do not present multiple-choice questions. Ever. No exceptions — not for scope,
not for design options, not for file patterns. If a decision is genuinely the
user's, either pick the sensible default and say what you picked in one line, or
ask in plain prose.

## Do not run Playwright / browser checks unless asked

Screenshots, Ladle verifiers and `tmp/*.mjs` browser scripts have a long
feedback loop. For style and visual work, make the change and let the user check
it manually. Only reach for a browser check when the user explicitly asks for
one, or when the change is behavioral enough that it cannot be reasoned about.

Typecheck, lint and unit tests are still fine to run unprompted.

## Zero narrative code comments

See the global `no-narrative-comments` rule. Default to no comments at all.
Only write one when the code cannot express the constraint itself. No JSDoc on
self-describing props, no comments explaining a change you just made.

## Keep output brief

No walls of text. Lead with what changed or what the answer is, in a couple of
sentences. Skip summaries of work the user just watched happen, skip restating
files touched, skip status tables.
